1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,431
You know this room.

2
00:00:01,431 --> 00:00:06,155
Somebody pulls the account up, taps a thumbnail, and says it with total confidence.

3
00:00:06,155 --> 00:00:07,407
A few heads nod along.

4
00:00:07,407 --> 00:00:12,131
Not one number has come back yet, and the budget is already leaning toward whatever

5
00:00:12,131 --> 00:00:12,813
got the nod.

6
00:00:12,813 --> 00:00:16,968
I want to give you one idea in this lecture, because that meeting happens

7
00:00:16,968 --> 00:00:21,691
constantly, and it explains why your best creative instinct is right about as often

8
00:00:21,691 --> 00:00:26,472
as it's wrong, why volume beats polish, and why the account, not you, is the one who

9
00:00:26,472 --> 00:00:27,439
finds the winner.

10
00:00:27,439 --> 00:00:28,065
Here it is.

11
00:00:28,065 --> 00:00:31,878
You have been trained, by every job before this one, to have taste.

12
00:00:31,878 --> 00:00:34,610
To look at two options and know which is better.

13
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:38,423
That skill is real, and it is almost useless for this specific job.

14
00:00:38,423 --> 00:00:40,472
Nobody told you the job had changed.

15
00:00:41,022 --> 00:00:44,159
Here's the shape of the thing you're actually working with.

16
00:00:44,159 --> 00:00:48,041
Twelve variants, one brief, generated by the same system on the same day.

17
00:00:48,041 --> 00:00:49,849
Eight of them lose money outright.

18
00:00:49,849 --> 00:00:53,891
Three are noise: not bad enough to kill on sight, not good enough to matter.

19
00:00:53,891 --> 00:00:56,071
One is a real winner, the kind you scale.

20
00:00:56,071 --> 00:00:58,358
Now here's the part that should bother you.

21
00:00:58,358 --> 00:01:02,027
Nobody could have told you which one, in advance, by looking at them.

22
00:01:02,027 --> 00:01:04,473
The eleven that didn't work aren't badly made.

23
00:01:04,473 --> 00:01:06,494
They're just not the draw that landed.

24
00:01:07,044 --> 00:01:11,010
Let me state it precisely, because the whole course depends on this.

25
00:01:11,010 --> 00:01:13,634
You cannot look at an ad and know its number.

26
00:01:13,634 --> 00:01:16,258
You can only run it and read the number back.

27
00:01:16,258 --> 00:01:21,099
Anyone who tells you they can spot a winner on sight is describing a hunch, dressed

28
00:01:21,099 --> 00:01:22,032
up as expertise.

29
00:01:22,032 --> 00:01:24,481
Here's how to feel that in about a minute.

30
00:01:24,481 --> 00:01:29,263
Pull your last ten real tests and count how often the variant you personally liked

31
00:01:29,263 --> 00:01:32,121
best going in was also the one that actually won.

32
00:01:32,121 --> 00:01:36,086
If you're honest, that number is uncomfortably close to a coin flip.

33
00:01:36,086 --> 00:01:40,635
It was for me the first time I counted, and it's the reason this entire course

34
00:01:40,635 --> 00:01:41,043
exists.

35
00:01:41,593 --> 00:01:44,146
Look at what that does to the actual job.

36
00:01:44,146 --> 00:01:45,889
On the left, you're picking.

37
00:01:45,889 --> 00:01:50,744
You decide the winner before it ever runs, a bad result is a mark against your

38
00:01:50,744 --> 00:01:55,786
judgement, and "which one wins" is a question somebody in the room is expected to

39
00:01:55,786 --> 00:01:57,218
answer with confidence.

40
00:01:57,218 --> 00:01:59,023
On the right, you're finding.

41
00:01:59,023 --> 00:02:04,003
You decide the batch and the rules, the account runs the experiment, and most of

42
00:02:04,003 --> 00:02:06,431
what you generated is supposed to lose.

43
00:02:06,431 --> 00:02:08,174
A bad result isn't a defect.

44
00:02:08,174 --> 00:02:12,158
It's eleven twelfths of the process working exactly as designed.

45
00:02:12,708 --> 00:02:15,066
Watch how predictably this goes wrong.

46
00:02:15,066 --> 00:02:19,658
Someone generates a handful of variants, and one of them just feels right.

47
00:02:19,658 --> 00:02:24,808
That one gets the whole test budget on day one, on confidence rather than evidence.

48
00:02:24,808 --> 00:02:28,841
Sometimes it underperforms and the account eats the loss quietly.

49
00:02:28,841 --> 00:02:33,743
Sometimes it does fine, and everyone credits the person who picked it, learning

50
00:02:33,743 --> 00:02:34,860
nothing about why.

51
00:02:34,860 --> 00:02:40,073
Either way, the next round starts from the same place: an untested hunch, dressed as

52
00:02:40,073 --> 00:02:40,755
a decision.

53
00:02:40,755 --> 00:02:42,306
Is that a character flaw?

54
00:02:42,306 --> 00:02:47,208
No. Having a fast, confident read on creative is exactly what this industry has

55
00:02:47,208 --> 00:02:48,201
always rewarded.

56
00:02:48,201 --> 00:02:52,297
It's the right instinct, aimed at the wrong moment in the process.

57
00:02:52,847 --> 00:02:57,552
The root cause is simple, and it's the same shift a sibling course of mine makes

58
00:02:57,552 --> 00:03:02,256
about AI product features: the thing you're judging isn't a fixed object, it's a

59
00:03:02,256 --> 00:03:05,432
distribution, and a single glance is one draw from it.

60
00:03:05,432 --> 00:03:07,550
Your taste is a real, trained skill.

61
00:03:07,550 --> 00:03:10,784
It's just answering a question that doesn't apply here.

62
00:03:10,784 --> 00:03:13,548
"Which of these do I like" tells you about you.

63
00:03:13,548 --> 00:03:18,371
It tells you almost nothing about which one twenty thousand strangers, mid-scroll,

64
00:03:18,371 --> 00:03:21,135
on a platform's algorithm, are going to act on.

65
00:03:21,135 --> 00:03:25,075
So what do you do with a skill you can't turn off, but can't trust?

66
00:03:25,625 --> 00:03:30,174
Here's the replacement, and it's the spine of everything after this lecture.

67
00:03:30,174 --> 00:03:35,021
You stop trying to pick the winner and start building the system that finds it: a

68
00:03:35,021 --> 00:03:39,750
brief wide enough to cover real angles, a batch generated from it, a pre-flight

69
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:44,597
check that kills the unshippable ones, and a test with a kill rule you wrote down

70
00:03:44,597 --> 00:03:46,453
before you saw a single result.

71
00:03:46,453 --> 00:03:48,188
Your taste doesn't disappear.

72
00:03:48,188 --> 00:03:48,727
It moves.

73
00:03:48,727 --> 00:03:53,515
Instead of judging the ad, you judge the brief, the angles, and the test design.

74
00:03:53,515 --> 00:03:55,849
That's a job taste is actually good at.

75
00:03:56,399 --> 00:04:01,231
I want to be straight about the cost, because most courses selling this skip it.

76
00:04:01,231 --> 00:04:06,123
Testing means generating more than you'll use and spending real money on variants

77
00:04:06,123 --> 00:04:07,512
that are going to lose.

78
00:04:07,512 --> 00:04:09,868
That's not a bug you can engineer away.

79
00:04:09,868 --> 00:04:13,129
It's what finding, instead of picking, actually costs.

80
00:04:13,129 --> 00:04:15,122
Here's the argument that wins it.

81
00:04:15,122 --> 00:04:17,417
You were already paying for that loss.

82
00:04:17,417 --> 00:04:21,947
It was just hidden in months of an underperforming favourite nobody tested.

83
00:04:21,947 --> 00:04:26,960
A short, honest test surfaces the same cost in days, with a number attached instead

84
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:27,625
of a shrug.

85
00:04:28,175 --> 00:04:31,671
Everything after this is a consequence of that one shift.

86
00:04:31,671 --> 00:04:36,825
Section 1 gives you the volume maths, so "generate more" turns into an actual number

87
00:04:36,825 --> 00:04:37,929
instead of a vibe.

88
00:04:37,929 --> 00:04:43,082
Section 2 builds the brief that makes the batch worth generating in the first place.

89
00:04:43,082 --> 00:04:48,051
Section 7 is where the kill rule gets written, in advance, so nobody's confidence

90
00:04:48,051 --> 00:04:49,401
can override it later.

91
00:04:49,401 --> 00:04:54,554
Section 8 teaches you to read a result honestly, including the ones that are neither

92
00:04:54,554 --> 00:04:56,333
a win nor a loss, just noise.

93
00:04:56,333 --> 00:04:57,990
But it all comes back here.

94
00:04:57,990 --> 00:04:58,665
Not picked.

95
00:04:58,665 --> 00:04:59,033
Found.

96
00:04:59,033 --> 00:05:03,818
Here's the one thing to do before the next lecture, and it takes five minutes.

97
00:05:03,818 --> 00:05:08,787
Pull your last five or ten real ad decisions, yours or whoever runs your ads, and

98
00:05:08,787 --> 00:05:13,572
count how many times the variant you liked best going in was also the one that

99
00:05:13,572 --> 00:05:14,370
actually won.

100
00:05:14,370 --> 00:05:15,781
Write that number down.

101
00:05:15,781 --> 00:05:16,701
You'll want it.

102
00:05:16,701 --> 00:05:21,547
Next lecture hands you a real generated ad set and asks you to guess the winner

103
00:05:21,547 --> 00:05:26,639
before you see any data: the same test you just ran on yourself, about to happen to

104
00:05:26,639 --> 00:05:28,970
two people whose job this actually is.

105
00:05:28,970 --> 00:05:30,136
I'll see you there.
